## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design in-place sorting algorithms, understand stability, and reason about trade-offs between time and memory in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts

- In-place merging
- Stability
- Time/space complexity
- Rotation-based merging

## Code Example

```javascript
function inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right) {
  let i = left, j = mid + 1;
  while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
    if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) { i++; continue; }
    const tmp = arr[j];
    for (let k = j; k > i; k--) arr[k] = arr[k - 1];
    arr[i] = tmp;
    i++; mid++; j++;
  }
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left, mid);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right);
  inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize rotations to guarantee O(n log n) time in all cases?
- How does memory locality affect performance compared with standard mergesort?

## Why This Is Asked
Autocompletion is common in editors and apps; this tests a lightweight data structure for fast prefix queries and shows practical space/time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Trie structure with per-node prefix counts
- O(len(prefix)) lookup
- Space proportional to total characters in dictionary
- Edge cases: empty prefix, missing path

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.count = 0;
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.count++;
    }
  }
  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support deletions of words while maintaining correct counts?
- How would you extend to Unicode or larger alphabets while preserving performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to fuse graph shortest-path ideas with node activation delays and cycle handling, plus choosing between DAG DP and general graph Dijkstra.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra with transformed edge costs w(u,v)+d(v)
- Topological sort for DAG optimization
- Handling multiple sources S
- Non-negative weights guarantee correctness

## Code Example
```javascript
function earliestFinish(V, adj, S, d) {
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = new Array(V).fill(INF);
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
  for (const s of S) { dist[s] = d[s]; pq.push([dist[s], s]); }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const [du, u] = pq.pop();
    if (du !== dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      const cand = dist[u] + w + d[v];
      if (cand < dist[v]) { dist[v] = cand; pq.push([cand, v]); }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

