## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine topo sort with DP and counting paths; ensures handling of unreachable nodes and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting
- DP on DAG
- Shortest path counting modulo
- Edge relaxation and correctness sketch

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathsInDAG(n, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>[]);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u,v,w] of edges) { adj[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++; }
  const topo = [];
  const q = [];
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  for (let i=0;i<q.length;i++) {
    const u = q[i]; topo.push(u);
    for (const [v] of adj[u]) if (--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);
  }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const ways = Array(n).fill(0);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  dist[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u]===INF) continue;
    for (const [v,w] of adj[u]) {
      const nd = dist[u] + w;
      if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; }
      else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD; }
    }
  }
  return {dist, ways};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this if edges can have negative weights but the graph remains a DAG? 
- How to extend to output the K smallest distances with counts efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
In practice, data often contains many duplicates. A 3-way partitioned quicksort minimizes comparisons on duplicates and scales with the number of distinct keys.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- Randomized pivot to avoid worst-case
- Complexity: O(N log D) expected when D distinct keys
- In-place sort with minimal extra space

## Code Example
```javascript
function sort3(a, l=0, r=a.length-1){
  if (l >= r) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random()*(r-l+1))+l];
  let i=l, lt=l, gt=r;
  while (i <= gt){
    if (a[i] < pivot){ [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (a[i] > pivot){ [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  sort3(a, l, lt-1);
  sort3(a, gt+1, r);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for stable sorting with duplicates?
- What are the trade-offs vs a counting/radix approach on integers?

